From: usdtweak <noreply@example.com>
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] cocoa: skip cursor warps while in GLFW_CURSOR_DISABLED

Removes every call to _glfwCenterCursorInContentArea() that fires while
the window is in GLFW_CURSOR_DISABLED mode.  The original code warped
the OS cursor to the window center in five different paths:

  1. updateCursorMode() — on entry to disabled mode
  2. windowDidResize:   — every time the window is resized
  3. windowDidMove:     — every time the window is moved
  4. windowDidBecomeKey: — every time the window regains focus
  5. (also called from line 1659 via _glfwSetCursorModeCocoa, but that
     path now hits the patched updateCursorMode)

CGAssociateMouseAndMouseCursorPosition(false) (called from
updateCursorMode immediately after the now-skipped centering) decouples
hardware mouse motion from the OS cursor position.  The cursor cannot
drift to a screen edge or otherwise misbehave, so centering is not
required for correctness — only for the visual position of the
(invisible) cursor in disabled mode.

Each centering call was the source of a one-shot giant delta because
the warp races with mouseMoved: events that the OS queued just before
the warp completed.  Within usdtweak, paths 1, 4 (focus regain) were
visibly hit during normal use: the entry case on every Fly-camera
engagement, and the focus case on system gestures, dock hovers, and
app switches.  See GLFW issues:
  - https://github.com/glfw/glfw/issues/2523 (this exact symptom)
  - https://github.com/glfw/glfw/issues/1523
  - https://github.com/glfw/glfw/issues/1269
  - https://github.com/glfw/glfw/issues/759
  - https://github.com/glfw/glfw/issues/193

Restoring the original cursor position when leaving disabled mode
(_glfwSetCursorPosCocoa(restoreCursorPosX/Y) in updateCursorMode's
else branch) still works correctly because restoreCursorPosX/Y is
captured before any (now-skipped) warp.

--- a/src/cocoa_window.m
+++ b/src/cocoa_window.m
@@ -92,7 +92,16 @@
         _glfwGetCursorPosCocoa(window,
                                &_glfw.ns.restoreCursorPosX,
                                &_glfw.ns.restoreCursorPosY);
-        _glfwCenterCursorInContentArea(window);
+        // NOTE: Do NOT warp the cursor to the content area center on entry.
+        //       CGAssociateMouseAndMouseCursorPosition(false) below decouples
+        //       hardware motion from cursor position, so the cursor cannot
+        //       drift to a screen edge and centering is unnecessary.
+        //       The unconditional warp was the source of GLFW #1523 / #2523:
+        //       it raced with mouseMoved: events queued before the warp,
+        //       producing a one-shot giant delta on the first event after
+        //       entering disabled mode. (See SDL3 SDL_cocoamouse.m for the
+        //       same pattern, which also warps but applies position-anchored
+        //       compensation; the simplest fix is to skip the warp entirely.)
         CGAssociateMouseAndMouseCursorPosition(false);
     }
     else if (_glfw.ns.disabledCursorWindow == window)
@@ -231,8 +240,12 @@
     if (window->context.source == GLFW_NATIVE_CONTEXT_API)
         [window->context.nsgl.object update];
 
-    if (_glfw.ns.disabledCursorWindow == window)
-        _glfwCenterCursorInContentArea(window);
+    // NOTE: Do NOT re-center the cursor on resize while in disabled mode.
+    //       The cursor is decoupled via CGAssociateMouseAndMouseCursorPosition;
+    //       a mid-capture warp here would race with queued mouseMoved: events
+    //       and produce a one-shot giant delta (GLFW #2523, see also patch 0001).
+    // if (_glfw.ns.disabledCursorWindow == window)
+    //     _glfwCenterCursorInContentArea(window);
 
     const int maximized = [window->ns.object isZoomed];
     if (window->ns.maximized != maximized)
@@ -266,8 +279,10 @@
     if (window->context.source == GLFW_NATIVE_CONTEXT_API)
         [window->context.nsgl.object update];
 
-    if (_glfw.ns.disabledCursorWindow == window)
-        _glfwCenterCursorInContentArea(window);
+    // NOTE: Do NOT re-center the cursor on window-move while in disabled
+    //       mode.  Same rationale as the windowDidResize: case above.
+    // if (_glfw.ns.disabledCursorWindow == window)
+    //     _glfwCenterCursorInContentArea(window);
 
     int x, y;
     _glfwGetWindowPosCocoa(window, &x, &y);
@@ -292,8 +307,14 @@
 
 - (void)windowDidBecomeKey:(NSNotification *)notification
 {
-    if (_glfw.ns.disabledCursorWindow == window)
-        _glfwCenterCursorInContentArea(window);
+    // NOTE: Do NOT re-center the cursor on focus regain while in disabled
+    //       mode.  This was the dominant remaining source of mid-capture
+    //       jumps observed in usdtweak: any focus loss/regain (system
+    //       gesture, dock hover, app-switch glitch) would warp the cursor
+    //       and produce a one-shot giant delta against post-warp
+    //       virtualCursorPos.  Same rationale as patch 0001.
+    // if (_glfw.ns.disabledCursorWindow == window)
+    //     _glfwCenterCursorInContentArea(window);
 
     _glfwInputWindowFocus(window, GLFW_TRUE);
     updateCursorMode(window);
